{
"A lump of salt makes a cup undrinkable, but not the Ganges. The same deed lands differently on different people.": "c69369e230b5",
"Asked whether the one who acts is the one who reaps, the Buddha said neither the same nor another.": "b6b3cb42276a",
"He listed eight causes of what we feel, and past deeds were only one of them.": "e9f1f79514d8",
"It is not a verdict on who you are. It describes how you are becoming, and you have a hand in it.": "90e7f9326291",
"Kant accepted that every event in nature has a cause, and he still held that a rational being must regard itself as free. His solution was to distinguish two points of view. In T. K. Abbotts translation of the Groundwork, a rational being has two points of view from which he can regard himself. As part of the world of sense, it is subject to laws of nature. As a member of the intelligible world, it acts under laws that have their foundation not in experience but in reason alone. This resembles the Buddhist two truths applied to action. At one level everything is conditioned, and at another we speak of agents who choose and are responsible. The difference is where the freedom lives. Kant placed it in a self beyond experience that we cannot know but must assume. The Buddha denied any such self. For him the language of the responsible agent is conventional truth, and it is reliable and needed, because it describes a causal series that can be changed. It does not point to a free self standing behind the series.": "244f1ab82ee9",
"Karma is not a sentence. It is a process that present intention can redirect.": "ce67d5b5f0aa",
"Many Westerners hear karma as cosmic payback, and some hear it as a way of blaming victims. Correct both at once with three short texts. First, the Buddha defined karma as intention, so it is about the mind, not about luck. Second, he listed eight causes of illness and made karma only one of them, so it is wrong to blame anyones suffering on their past lives. Third, tell the story of the salt. It turns karma from a sentence into a process, and it puts the weight on what a person does now. If the audience knows the free will debate, add that the Buddhas position is a kind of compatibilism without a soul and without a God, and that Chrysippus cylinder is the nearest Western image.": "e6c21ed77c1a",
"The British philosopher Galen Strawson, son of the P. F. Strawson discussed in the lesson on free will, gave a short argument that he called the Basic Argument. You do what you do because of the way you are. To be ultimately responsible for what you do, you would have to be ultimately responsible for the way you are. But to make yourself the way you are, you would already have to be some way, with preferences that guided the making, and you would have to be responsible for those too. The regress never ends, so no one can be ultimately responsible for anything, whether or not determinism is true. A Buddhist can accept almost all of this. There is no self that creates itself, and the Buddhas account of conditions says much the same. The difference is in what follows. Strawson concluded that deep desert, the idea that people truly deserve heaven or hell, makes no sense. The Buddhist tradition kept karma, and dropped the self that would have to deserve its results. Results follow from intentions as fruit follows a seed. Nobody is sentenced. The practical question is never whether one deserves ones past, only what to plant now.": "a95c5eaddb54",
"The Buddha also denied that karma explains everything. When the wanderer Sīvaka repeats the view that everything this individual person experiences is because of past deeds, the Buddha lists eight sources of feeling: bile, phlegm, wind, their combination, change in weather, not taking care of yourself, exertion, and the result of deeds is the eighth (Saṁyutta Nikāya 36.21). Those who blame every experience on past deeds, he said, go beyond personal experience and beyond what is generally deemed to be true. Karma is one condition among many. And even where it works, it does not work like a fixed sentence. Suppose a person was to drop a lump of salt into a mug of water. Would that mug of water become salty and undrinkable? Yes. Suppose a person was to drop a lump of salt into the Ganges River. No, because the Ganges River is a vast mass of water (Aṅguttara Nikāya 3.100). The same small wrong deed can lead one person to hell and pass almost unnoticed in another, who has developed ethics, mind, and wisdom. The result depends on who has become what.": "3e9d07662f79",
"The Buddha changed the meaning of karma. In the religion around him the word meant ritual action, the correct performance of a sacrifice. He made it a matter of the mind. In Bhikkhu Sujatos translation he said, It is intention that I call deeds. For after making a choice one acts by way of body, speech, and mind (Aṅguttara Nikāya 6.63). The Treasury of Abhidharma puts this in a verse: The variety of the world is born of action, which is intention and what intention produces. Its commentary adds a point the Western debate usually takes for granted: the worlds variety is not produced by a single lord who first had the thought, but arises from the differences in beings actions. There is no God here whose plan might compete with human choice.": "31291e5e9aee",
"The Buddha said, It is intention that I call deeds. Karma is about the mind.": "955b12d19536",
"The Stoic Chrysippus held that everything happens by fate, an orderly series, established by nature, of all events, following one another and joined together from eternity. His critics said that this made punishment unjust. His reply survives in Aulus Gellius, in J. C. Rolfes translation: If you roll a cylindrical stone over a sloping, steep piece of ground, you do indeed furnish the beginning and cause of its rapid descent. After that it speeds on not because you make it do so, but because of its peculiar form and natural tendency to roll. Fate supplies the push, and the shape of ones mind decides the course. A well-formed mind meets the same pressure with little opposition and little difficulty, while a crude one falls into fault even under slight pressure. This is very close to the salt and the river. In both, the same outside condition yields different results depending on the character it meets. The difference is that for Chrysippus fate is the rational order of a divine cosmos, and the Buddha denied any such order.": "56c1fd9231b4",
"The closest is Chrysippus. Both accept that everything is caused and still find room for responsibility in the character that meets each cause. The cylinder and the river make the same point with different images. Kant is closest on the two levels of description, and Galen Strawson is closest in denying a self that could create itself. In the language of the lesson on free will, the Buddhas position is a form of compatibilism. Causation and responsible action fit together, and nothing in it depends on an uncaused will.": "0f688509114c",
"The first further step is that the Buddha took karma out of the accounting room. Western discussions of free will usually begin with blame. Can we punish someone whose act was caused? Popular Western ideas of karma make it a moral ledger, in which every wrong is recorded and repaid. The suttas resist both pictures. The question that interested the Buddha was not whether the past deserves punishment, but how a person can stop producing suffering. The salt and the river put the weight on the present. A person who develops ethics, mind, and wisdom becomes a river, and the same old deed lands differently. This is why the teaching on karma belongs with meditation and ethics and does not stand alone as a theory of justice. Karma explains why practice works.": "72833b856541",
"The likeness ends at two points. The first is the cosmos. The Stoics trusted a rational fate, and Kant needed God and an immortal soul as postulates of morality. The Buddhas world has neither. Karma is not produced by a single lord, and no plan stands behind it. The second is the aim. Western compatibilists want to show that praise and blame are justified. The Buddha wanted to show that change is possible, that a mug can become a river. His teaching on karma is mainly addressed to the person who acts, and it tells that person to train, more than it tells anyone how to judge others. The traditions practical summary is a reflection that everyone, lay or ordained, should often review: I am the owner of my deeds and heir to my deeds (Aṅguttara Nikāya 5.57). It is a vow to take care with what one plants, and it makes no verdict on anyones past.": "8d678d6c9372",
"The second step concerns the one who acts. Galen Strawson showed that no self can create itself, and he concluded that ultimate desert is impossible. Many Western readers find that conclusion bleak, as if it took the meaning out of effort. The Buddhist response is that effort never needed a self-made self. It needs only that intention conditions what comes next, and the suttas say it does. Removing the self also removes something that makes karma heavy, the sense that I am a fixed person who must pay. What remains is a series that can learn. For a teacher this is the most useful thing to say about karma to a Western student: it is not a verdict on who you are. It is a description of how you are becoming, and you have a hand in it.": "6db896e020f8",
"This raises the question the previous lesson left open. If every moment perishes, who receives the result of a deed? A brahmin asked exactly this: Are he who does the deed and he who experiences the result one and the same? The Buddha answered that saying they are the same is one extreme, and saying they are different is the other extreme. Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way, and he went on to recite dependent origination, beginning, in Sujatos rendering, Ignorance is a requirement for choices (Saṁyutta Nikāya 12.46). There is no lasting agent who acts and then waits for the bill. There is a causal series in which intentions shape what comes next, including the next intentions. The series is the continuity, and the Buddha would not reduce it to either sameness or difference.": "07810098a9da"
}
